.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "int8_t csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::ANSYS_Type (const std::string & FEtype)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns the ANSYS integer flag corresponding to the ANSYS element name string\&. 
.SS "\fBCSMP_FEM_TYPE\fP csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::CSMP_TypeFrom_ANSYS_Type (int8_t etype, bool isoparametric, uint32_t dim)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns the CSMP finite element type enum identifier that corresponds to the ANSYS type as identified by an integer identifier\&. The user needs to specify whether an isoparametric or globally interpolated element shall be returned because ANSYS elements can be converted into both\&. 
.br

.PP
Note however that ANSYS fits quadratic or cubic elements to the bounding curves\&. Thus, global interpolation will give incorrect results as it works only for straight-sided elements\&. 
.SS "\fBCSMP_FEM_TYPE\fP csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::CSMP_TypeFrom_ANSYS_TypeName (const std::string & ANSYS_element_type, bool isoparametric, uint32_t dim)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns the CSMP finite element type enum identifier that corresponds to the ANSYS type as identified by a character string\&. The user needs to specify whether an isoparametric or globally interpolated element shall be returned because ANSYS elements can be converted into both\&. 
.br

.PP
Note however that ANSYS fits quadratic or cubic elements to the bounding curves\&. Thus, global interpolation will give incorrect results as it works only for straight-sided elements\&. 
.SS "std::string csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::CSMP_TypeNameFrom_ANSYS_Type (int8_t etype, bool isoparametric, uint32_t dim)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns the CSP finite element type name that corresponds to the ANSYS type as identified by an integer identifier\&. The user needs to specify whether an isoparametric or globally interpolated element shall be returned because ANSYS elements can be converted into both\&. 
.br

.PP
Note however that ANSYS fits quadratic or cubic elements to the bounding curves\&. Thus, global interpolation will give incorrect results as it works only for straight-sided elements\&. 
.SS "bool csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::CubicElement (int8_t etype)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns whether the ANSYS element type specifier denotes a finite element type that uses cubic basis functions\&. ANSYS element types with such characteristics are: 
.br

.PP
.PP
.nf
cubic
==============
HEXA_32     32
.fi
.PP
 
.SS "size_t csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::InterpolationOrder (const std::string & etype)\fR [static]\fP"
Method deduces order of element interpolation functions from the input finite-element type\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The ANSYS type name of the finite element that shall be queried\&. 
.br

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
size_t giving the order of interpolation (1=linear, 2=quadratic etc\&.)
.RE
.PP
The interpolation order is returned as an unsigned integer value: 1 = linear, 2 = quadratic, 3 = cubic\&. 
.SS "bool csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::LinearElement (int8_t etype)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns whether the ANSYS element type specifier denotes a finite element type that uses linear basis functions\&. ANSYS element types with such characteristics are: 
.br

.PP
.PP
.nf
linear
==============
BAR_2        2
TETRA_4      4
HEXA_8       6
TRI_3        8
TRI_3_X      9
PENTA_6     12
QUAD_4      14
QUAD_4_X    15
PYRA_5      18
.fi
.PP
 
.SS "bool csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::LineElement (int8_t etype)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns true if the ANSYS input element type is a line\&. Possible ANSYS types are: 
.br

.PP
.PP
.nf
element type     identifier
#define BAR_2       2
#define BAR_3       3
.fi
.PP
 
.SS "void csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::LineElements (std::list< std::string > & line_elements)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns a list of line type elements that includes both the ANSYS types and the corresponding CSP types (see \fBFiniteElement\&.h\fP)\&. The output strings are: 
.br

.PP
.PP
.nf
ANSYS element type
BAR_2  BAR_3  BAR_4
CSMP types
LINEAR_BAR  
ISOPARAMETRIC_LINEAR_BAR
ISOPARAMETRIC_QUADRATIC_BAR
ISOPARAMETRIC_CUBIC_BAR
.fi
.PP
 
.SS "size_t csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::MinimumSpatialDimension (const std::string & ANSYS_finite_element_type)\fR [static]\fP"
The element type used defines the spatial dimension of the model\&. It is indicative only of the minimum dimension since lines and surfaces can also exist in 3D space, which the name makes explicit\&. 
.br
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
Method expects a string argument that contains the ANSYS element type to deduce the spatial dimension from\&. 
.br

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
size_t The minimum spatial dimension is returned as an unsigned integer ranging from 1(=1D) to 3(=3D)\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "size_t csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::NeighborsPerElementOfType (int8_t etype)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns the number of neighbors of the input ANSYS finite element type\&. 
.SS "size_t csmp::ANSYS_ElementSpecifications::NodesPerElementOfType (int8_t etype)\fR [static]\fP"
Returns the number of nodes of the ANSYS element type as identified by the ANSYS element number\&. 
.br

.PP
